WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.521
Safe deserialization is not complete when
the parser returns an object.

2
00:00:03.601 --> 00:00:06.424
The application still needs to decide
whether the data is expected,

3
00:00:06.504 --> 00:00:08.187
complete, authorized, and meaningful.

4
00:00:08.267 --> 00:00:10.000
Use request DTOs, message DTOs, schemas,

5
00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:12.358
or explicit input models to define the
expected shape.

6
00:00:12.438 --> 00:00:15.558
Validate required fields, types, lengths,
ranges, formats, enum values,

7
00:00:15.638 --> 00:00:19.006
nested structures, array sizes, and
version fields before business logic runs.

8
00:00:19.086 --> 00:00:21.661
A public JSON request should usually
become a small DTO first,

9
00:00:21.741 --> 00:00:23.177
not a fully trusted entity object.

10
00:00:23.257 --> 00:00:26.723
A queue message should describe one
business event with a known schema and version.

11
00:00:26.803 --> 00:00:29.329
A file import should have clear column or
field definitions.

12
00:00:29.409 --> 00:00:32.578
Reject unsupported message types, unknown
versions, malformed payloads,

13
00:00:32.658 --> 00:00:36.273
oversized objects, unexpected structures,
and values that do not match the workflow.

14
00:00:36.353 --> 00:00:39.523
Validation protects application
assumptions, not just parser stability.

15
00:00:39.603 --> 00:00:41.237
Schema validation supports security,

16
00:00:41.317 --> 00:00:43.694
but it does not replace authentication,
authorization,

17
00:00:43.774 --> 00:00:46.646
object ownership checks, tenant checks, or
business-state checks.

18
00:00:46.726 --> 00:00:50.044
A payload can be well formed and still
request an action the caller is not allowed

19
00:00:50.124 --> 00:00:50.619
to perform.

20
00:00:50.699 --> 00:00:53.720
A message can pass schema validation and
still refer to the wrong tenant.

21
00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:57.019
A DTO can contain a valid status value
that is invalid for the current workflow

22
00:00:57.099 --> 00:00:57.396
state.

23
00:00:57.476 --> 00:00:59.853
Business rules must be enforced after
shape validation.

24
00:00:59.933 --> 00:01:03.103
Avoid mass assignment by mapping only
allowed fields into domain objects or

25
00:01:03.183 --> 00:01:04.074
persistence models.

26
00:01:04.154 --> 00:01:07.670
Keep public API shapes separate from
internal domain objects when sensitive fields

27
00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:08.047
exist.

28
00:01:08.127 --> 00:01:11.297
Version serialized messages so producers
and consumers can evolve safely.

29
00:01:11.377 --> 00:01:14.645
Explicit mapping gives developers a place
to review which external fields are

30
00:01:14.725 --> 00:01:18.340
allowed to influence internal state and
which fields must remain server-controlled.
